1* Scalability :
  Ability of a system to handle increased load by adding resources, without fundamental changes to the architecture. A scalable system maintains performance (latency, throughput) as demand grows. 
  Vertical Scaling vs Horizontal Scaling 
  - Bigger machine             - More machines
  - More CPU, RAM, SSD         - Add server instances
  - Limit : hardware max       - Limit : almost none
  - Single point of failure    - Need load balancer and stateless design

    Stateless Services : The foundation of horizontal scaling is statelessness - any instance can handle any request as no request specific state lives on the server 
    - Session in Redis or encoded in JWT
    - Files in S3 or object storage
    - Shared cache (Redis cluster)
    - Websocket with pub-sub backplane

    Auto Scaling : adjust the number of instances based on metrics
                              Scale Out Trigger              Scale In Trigger 
        CPU utilization        > 70% for 3 min                < 30% for 10 min
        Request count          > 1000 RPS per instance        < 200 RPS per instance
        Queue depth            > 1000 messages pending        < 100 messages pending
        Response time          p95 > 500ms                    p95 < 100ms

    Cool down period : wait 5 - 10 min between scaling events to avoid thrashing
    Min/ Max instances : set bounds (min=2 for redundancy, max=50 for cost)
    Predictive scaling : Pre-scale before knowen traffic spikes (e.g Black Friday)

    Scaling the Database : 
      Single DB => Vertical Scale => Read Replicas => Cache Layer => Sharding => Multi-region
                        Handles             Techinique
    Single DB           < 1k QPS             one powerful machine
    Vertical scale      1 - 5k QPS           Bigger machine (more RAM, faster SSD)
    Read Replicas       10 - 50k read QPS    Route reads to replicas, write to primary
    Cache layer         100k+ read QPS       Redis/ Memcached absorbs 90%+ of reads
    Sharding            50k+ write QPS       Split data across multiple DB instances
    Multi-region        Global scale         Replicate across regions for low latency

    Real world Numbers (order of magnitude)
                        Single Instance Capacity
    Nhinx/ HAProxy       ~50k - 100k concurrent connections 
    Application server   500 - 5k RPS (depends on work per request)
    PostgreSQL           5k - 20k QPS (depends on query complexity)
    MySQL                5k - 30k QPS
    Redis                100k - 200k ops/sec (single thread)
    Kafka                500k - 2M messages/sec per broker
    Elasticsearch        1k - 10k queries/sec per node

    Scaling the Cache
                          When                         How
    Single Redis          < 100k ops/sec                One instance handles everything
    Redis cluster         100k - 1M+ ops/sec            Shard keys across multiple nodes
    Multi layer cache     Mixed access patterns         L1 in process -> L2 Redis -> L3 CDN
    Read replicas         Read heavy, geo distributed   Redis replicas in each region

    Scaling Strategies
                           Complexity     Cost               Best for
    Vertical scaling       Low            high per unit     Quick fix, databases
    Horizontal scaling     Medium         Linear            Stateless app tier
    Caching                Low - Medium   Low               Read heavy worklaods
    Read Replicas          Medium         Medium            Read heavy databases
    Sharding               High           Medium            Write heavy databases
    CDN                    Low            Low               static content, global users
    Async processing       Medium         Low               Spiky workloads

    When to use which 
      Vertical scaling when 
        - early stage, low traffic (<1k RPS)
        - Database thats hard to shard
        - Quick fix while planning horizontal approach
        - Workload is CPU/ memory bound on a single process
      Horizontal scaling when 
        - Expecting growth beyond single machine limits
        - Need high availability (no single point of failure)
        - Workload is parallelizable
        - Cost efficiency matters at scale
      Common mistakes 
        - Premature sharding before trying read replicas + caching
        - Scaling out stateful services without extracting state first
        - Not setting auto scaling cooldown periods (leads to thrashing)
        - Ignoring the database bottlenect while scaling the app tier

Q1. How would you scale a system from 100 users to 10M users ?
    Progressive approach: Each step is triggered by a specific bottleneck, not done preemptively
      1. Start with a single server + DB 
      2. Seperate app and DB to different machines 
      3. Add a load balancer + multiple app servers
      4. Add Redis cache to reduce DB load
      5. Add read replicas for read heavy queries
      6. CDN for static content
      7. Shard the databases when write throughput is the bottleneck
      8. Add message queues for async work
      9. Multi region for global latency

Q2. What's the difference between scaling out and scaling up for databases?
    Scaling up (vertical) means a bigger DB machine - more RAM means more data fits in the buffer pool, faster CPUs handle more queries. Its simple but has limits and creates a single point of failure.
    Scaling out (horizontal) means sharding - splitting data across multiple DB instances. Each shard handle a subset of the data. Its complex (nrrds shard keys, cross shard queries are expensive) but practically unlimited.

Q3. How do you make a service stateless ?
    Identify all server - local state: sessions, caches, file uploads, Websocket connections. 
    Move sessions to Redis or use JWTs. 
    Move caches to a shared Redis cluster.
    For Websockets, use a pub-sub backplane (Redis pub/sub) so any server can push to any client.

Q4. When is vertical scaling the right choice ?
    For databases in the early to mid stage (sharding is complex and premature optimization)
    For batch processing jobs that benifit from more RAM/ CPU on a single node.
    For systems where the engineering cost of horizontal scaling exceeds the hardware cost of a bigger machine

2* Load Balancing :
    A load balancer distributes incoming requests across multiple servers so no single server gets overwhelmed
    - High availability, Scalibility, Performace, Maintenance
    - You can have LBs at every layer — external (user-facing), internal (service-to-service), and even DB-level (read replicas).
    
    Load Balancing Algorithms :
      - Round Robin : rotate through servers in order
      - Weighted Round Robin : same as round robin but stronger server gets more traffic. (when some servers has more CPU/ RAM)
      - Least connections : send to server with fewest sctive connections right now
      - Least Response Time : send to the server with the fastest recent response time + fewest connections (when latency matters more than pure load distribution)
      - IP hash : Hash the clients IP to determine which server to use. (same client always hits the same server)
      - Consistent hashing : Advanced version of IP hashing that minimizes disruption when servers are added/ removed. Only K/N keys needs to be remapped (K = Keys, N = Nodes). (Distributed caches, databases sharding)

  Algorithm Comparison
                            Even Distribution     Session Affinity    Adapts to load    complexity
    Round Robin              Yes                    No                  No                Very Low
    Weighted RR              Configurable           No                  No                Low
    Least connections        Adaptive               No                  Yes               Medium
    IP Hash                  Depends on IPs         Yes                 No                Low
    Consistent Hashing       Good                   Yes                 No                Medium

   L4 vs L7 Load Balancing 
                          L4 (Transport)                  L7 (Applications)
    Sees                  IP + Port + TCP/ UDP            Full HTTP: URL, headers, cookie, body
    Decision based on     Source/ dest Ip and port        URL paths, headers, cookies, content
    Speed                 Faster (less parsing)           Slower (inspects full request)
    Features              Basic distribution              Path routing, header - based routing auth
    SSL                   Passes through (or terminates)  Always terminates, can inspect
    Example               AWS NLB, HAProxy TCP mode       AWS ALB, Ngnix, Envoy

  In interviews, L7 is almost always what you want — you need path-based routing for microservices.

  Health Check
    Active : LB periodically pings each server (most common) 
    Passive : LB monitors real traffic. If a server returns 5xx for N consecutive requests, mark it unhealthy
  
  Health check endpoints should verify 
    - server process is running 
    - can connect to database
    - can connect to Redis
    - Disk space is okay

  Sticky sessions 
  User logs into Server A and Next requet goes to Server B
                                      How                                      Pros                                 Cons
  External session store (Redis)      Store sessions in shared Redis           stateless servers, best approach     Extra infrastructure
  Sticky sessions (cookie based)      LB routes same user to same server       Simple                               server death = lost sessions
  JWT Tokens                          session data in the token itself         No server storage needed             cant revoke easily, token size

  * Best practice: Store sessions in Redis. Servers are stateless. LB uses any algorithm without worrying about session affinity.

  Load Balancing at scale 
      Single LB is a single point of failure. Solutions:
      Active-Passive: Two LBs. Primary handles all traffic. If it dies, passive takes over (via floating IP/DNS failover).
      Active-Active: Both LBs handle traffic (DNS returns both IPs). If one dies, the other handles all.
      DNS Load Balancing: Multiple LB IPs in DNS. Client picks one. Coarse-grained but simple.

Q1. What load balancing algorithm would you use here ?
    Least connections for most cases (adapts ti varying request times). Round t=robin if all requests are similar duraction. IP Hash or Consistent Hashing if you need session affinity or cache locality
Q2. How do you handle a server going down ? 
    Health checks every 10 seconds. After 2 - 3 consecutive failures, mark server as unhealthy and stop routing traffic to it. Alert the team. Auto - scaling replaces it.  
Q3. Whats the difference between L4 and L7 ?
    L4 routes based on IP/ port (fast, simple). L7 reads the full HTTP request adn routes based on URL path, headers, or cookies (flexible, needed for microservices)
Q4. Can the load balancer become a bottleneck ?
    Yes, if its a single instance. Solutions: DNS- based multi- LB, active pair, or scaling the LB horizontally (AWS ALB auto-scales).

    Read World Tools 
                    Type                 Best For
    AWS ALB         L7, managed          Most web apps, microservices
    AWS NLB         L4, managed          Ultra low latency, TCP/ UCP
    Nginx           L7, self managed     Reverse proxy + LB combo
    HA proxy        L4/ L7 self managed  High performance, fine control
    Envoy           L7, sidecar          Service mesh (istio)
    Cloudflare      L7, edge             CDN + LB + DDoS (distributed denial of service) protection

3* CDN - content delivery network :
  A CDN is a network of servers distributed worldwide (edge servers) that cache and serve content from the location nearest to the user.
  How it works :
    First request (cache miss) : User -> CDN doesnt have -> Origin server -> CDN Edge caches the image locally -> Returns to the user (slow this one time).
    Subsequent request (cache HIT) : user -> CDN Edge -> Returns to the user immediately (fast).

  Pull CDN
    CDN fetches content from origin only when first requested. If no one requests it, it's never cached at that edge.
  Push CDN
    Explicitly upload content to CDN edge servers. CDN serves it without ever contacting your origin.
  Which to Use ?
                                            Best Approach
    Static assets (css, js, images)          Pull CDN
    User - uploaded media (profile pics)     Pull CDN
    Popular video content                    Push CDN
    Live streaming                           Specialized CDN (not cached)
    API responses                            Usually Not CDN (dynamic), unless cacheable

  What to Cache on CDN :
    Cache                                    Dont Cache
    Static files (css, js, fonts)            User specific data (dashboard, settings)
    Images, videos, pdfs                     Authentication tokens
    Public API responses (product catalog)   Real - time data (stock prices, chat)
    HTML pages (for anonymous users)         POST/ PUT/ DELETE responses

  Cache Invalidation on CDN 
    On content update, how to make the CDN serve the new version ?
      Strategy 1. Versioned URLs (Best)
          CDN caches indefinitely. New deployment = new URL. Old URL still works fro users with old HTML
                  v1: /static/app.v1.js
                  v2: /static/app.v2.js (new URL = CDN treats as new file = no stale cache)
      Strategy 2. Cache - Control headers
                  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400  (cache for 24 hours)
                  Cache-Control: no-cache  (always revalidate with origin)
                  Cache-Control: no-store  (never cache)
      Strategy 3. Purge API
          Most CDNs offer an API to force-invalidate cached content. Trade-off: Purge takes time to propagate (seconds to minutes across all edges).
                  POST /purge {"urls": ["https://example.com/image.png"]} → CDN removes from all edges → next request fetches fresh from origin
  
  CDN Performance Numbers
        Metric                              Without CDN        With CDN
      Latency (US user, US server)          50ms                 10ms
      Latency (India user, US server)       200ms                15ms (served from India edge)
      Origin server load                    100% of requests     5 - 10 % (90%+ served by CDN)
      Bandwidth cost                        High                 Lower (CDN absorbs most traffic)
      Availability during origin outage     0%                   Partial (cached content still served)
  
  CDN Providers
        Provider                  Best For                       Unique feature
      Cloudflare                General web, DDoS protection    Free tier, Workers (edge compute)
      AWS CloudFront            AWS ecosystem                   Tight S3 integration, Lambda@Edge
      Fastly                    Real-time purging               Instant purge (< 150ms globally)
      Akamai                    Enterprise, largest network     300k+ edge servers
      Google Cloud CDN          GCP ecosystem                   Global anycast, load balancing

  Edge computing (CDN + Logic)
    Modern CDNs can run code at the edge (not just cache static files)
        Traditional : User -> CDN (static only) -> Origin (all logic)
        Edge computing : User -> CDN Edge (runs simple logic like auth, redirects, A/B testing) -> Origin (only for DB queries)
    Examples :
      Cloudflare workers
      AWS Lambda@Edge / CloudFront Functions
      Fastly Compute@Edge
    Use Cases:
      URL redirects at the edge (no origin hit)
      A/B testing (decide variant at edge)
      Auth token validation (reject unauthenticated requests before hitting origin)
      Image resizing/ optimization at edge
  
  CDN in System Design interviews - When to mention CDN ?
    - System servers static content (images, videos, css/js)
    - Users are globally distributed
    - Read - heavy workload with cacheable responses
    - Need to reduce origin server load
  Example :
      “User-uploaded images are stored in S3. We put CloudFront in front of S3 so images are served from the nearest edge location. First request goes to origin, subsequent requests are served from cache with ~10ms latency. We use versioned URLs for cache busting.”

Q1. How does a CDN reduce latency ?
    Content is served from an edge server geographically close to the user (10-20ms) instead of travelling to the origin (100-300ms). Physics - speed of light through fiber has a floor.
Q2. What if the origin goes down ?
    CDN continues serving cached content. User see existing content but cant get new content. This is 'stale-while-error' better thana complete outage
Q3. How do you handle personalized content with a CDN ?
    Dont cache personalized content on CDN. Use CDN only for static/ public assets. Personalized API responses go directly to origin servers. 
    Or use edge computing to assemble personlized pages from cached fragments.
Q4. CDN vs Read Replica ?
    CDN caches the final rendered content (bytes). Read Replica caches at the database level (queries). CDN eliminates the server entirely for cached requests.
    Use both: CDN for static content, replicas for dynamic database queries.

4* Proxy :
    A proxy is an intermediary server that sits between a client and a destination server, forwarding requests on behalf of one side. It can modify, filter, cache, or log traffic passing through it.
    
    - Forward Proxy (Client side)
        A forward proxy sits in front of clients and forwards their requests to the internet. The destination server sees the proxy IP, not the clients
                Use Case               How
            Corporate filtering      Block access to certain websites
            Anonymity                Hide client IP from destination 
            Caching                  Cache frequently accessed content (reduce bandwidth)
            Access control           Require authentication before internet access
            Geo-bypass               Access content restricted to other regions (VPN)
            Logging                  Monitor all outbound traffic from an organization
      Example: Squid proxy, corporate firewalls, VPN services, Tor network

    - Reverse Proxy (Server side)
        A reverse proxy sits in front of servers and handles incoming requests from the internet. Clients see the proxy's address not the actual servers
              Use Case                 How
          Load balancing           Distribute requests across backend servers
          SSL termination          Handle TLS encryption/ decryption at the proxy
          Caching                  Cache static assets and API responses
          Compression              GZip responses before sending to client
          Security                 Hide backend topology, WAF, DDos protection
          A/B testing              Route percentage of traffic to different backends
          Canary deployments       Send 5% traffic to new version
      Example: Nginx, HAProxy, cloudflare, AWS ALB, Apace httpd

  Forward vs Reverse Proxy comparision
      Aspect                 Forward Proxy                      Reverse Proxy
    Works for                Client                              Server
    Hides                    Client Identity                     Server Identity
    Position                 Infront of clients                  Infront of servers
    Who configures           Client or clients network admin     Server owner
    Common Use               Corporate networks, VPN             Webservers, CDN, LB
    Client awareness         Client knows its using a proxy      Client doesnt know (transparent)

    - Transparent Proxy
        A transperent proxy intercepts traffic without the clients knowledge or configuration. The client doesnt know a proxy exists.
              Use cases
          ISP caching (Intercepting HTTP to serve cached content)
          Corporate content filtering without client configuration
          CDN edge caching (Intercepting DNS to route to nearest PoP)
          Government censorship (Intercepting and blocking specific domains)
        Limitation: Doesnt work well with HTTPS (cant inspect encrypted traffic without MITM certificate)
    
    - Sidecar Proxy (Service Mesh)
        A sidecar proxy runs alongside every service instance, handling all inbound and outbound network traffic transparently. The application communicates with localhost; the sidecar handles service discovery, TLS, retries, and observability.
            Envoy proxy features:
              L7 load balancing (HTTP/2, gRPC native)
              Automatic retries with circuit breaking
              mTLS between all services (zero-trust)
              Distributed tracing headers propagation
              Rate limiting (local and global)
              Hot restarts (update config without dropping connections)
        Used by: Istio (Envoy sidecar), Linkerd (linkerd-proxy), Consul connect (Envoy)

    Nginx as Reverse Proxy - most popular reverse proxy for web application
          Feature             Capability
        Load balancing      
        SSL/ TLS
        Caching
        Compression
        Rate limiting
        Health checks
        Websocket
        Throughput
        
    

5* API Gateway :
    An API gateway is a single entry point fro all client requests into a microservice architecture. It acts as a reverse proxy that routes requests to appropriate backend services while handling cross-cutting concerns like authentication, rate limiting, and response transformation.
        Without a gateway, every client must 
          - know the address of every microservice
          - Handle authentication with each service individually
          - Make multiple round trips to compose a single view
          - Deal with different protocols (gRPC, REST, GraphQL)
        Gateway Responsibilities
          - Authentication and Authorization               Validate JWT/ OAuth tokens before forwarding
          - Rate Limiting                                  Enforce per-user or per-IP request limits
          - Request Routing                                Map /api/user/* to user service, Map /api/orders/* to order service
          - Request caching                                Cache GET responses at the edge
          - Request Transformation/ Protocol Translation   Convert REST to gRPC, add headers, rewrite paths
          - Request Aggregation                            Combine responses from multiple services into one
          - SSL Termination                                Handle TLS at the gateway, use plain HTTP internally
          - Load balancing / Circuit Breaking              Stop forwarding to unhealthy services
          - Logging and Metrics / Observability            Centralized logging, tracing, metrics for all traffic

    Backend for Frontend (BFF) pattern
        Different clients have different needs. A mobile app needs compact responses; a web dashboard needs rich data. BFF provides a dedicated gateway per client type.
        BFF benefits:
          - optimized response payloads per client (mobile gets less data)
          - Differnet auth flows (mobile = OAuth + biometric, web = session cookies)
          - Independent deployment per client team
          - Can aggregate multiple backend calls into one client optimized response
      Used by: Netflix (per-device BFF), SoundCloud, Spotify

    
  
        
    
























  
  
